--TEST--
An enqueue into a deployment that configures no `[queue]` block is refused at the push, rather than writing a row nothing would ever claim — `rule:core-classes/queue-storage-is-a-table`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A queue fails silently by construction: a job nothing ever claims looks
// exactly like one whose turn has not come round. So the moment an enqueue can
// be known to be pointless is the moment it has to say so, and that moment is
// here — this deployment names a database for the *application*, and none for
// the jobs. `nvs_config::queue`'s module doc is the home of that reading, and
// this is the same judgement one call later, at the only place a request can
// still be told.
try {
    var $id = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs");
    echo "enqueued", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// ADR 0118 § 5's shape: the world refused a call, and the program is still
// running underneath it.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A database, and deliberately no `[queue]`. The block's own absence is how a
# deployment says it runs no jobs -- `rule:core-classes/queue-storage-is-a-table` -- so this is a valid tree
# and not a broken one, which is what makes the refusal the enqueue's own.
[db.main]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
--EXPECT--
Core\Queue::push: this deployment writes no `[queue]` block, so nothing names the database a job would live in — `rule:core-classes/queue-storage-is-a-table` is the block, and `connection` is the field
still running
